 {
  "id":"c-conditions-and-loops","title":"Conditions and Loops in C","track":"c","description":"Control program flow using if, switch, for and while.","difficulty":"beginner","xpReward":75,"prerequisites":["c-variables-and-types"],
  "steps":[
   {"id":"cl1","type":"explain","title":"Make decisions with if and else","content":"An if statement runs its block when its condition is nonzero (true). An optional else block runs when the condition is zero (false). Use braces to make blocks clear, even when they contain one statement.","code":"int score = 72;\nif (score >= 40) {\n    printf(\"Pass\\n\");\n} else {\n    printf(\"Try again\\n\");\n}","output":"Pass","explanation":"72 >= 40 is true, so the first block runs."},
   {"id":"cl2","type":"explain","title":"Repeat work with a for loop","content":"A for loop has an initialization, a condition, and an update expression. The condition is checked before each iteration. A loop from i = 1 while i <= 3 runs three times.","code":"#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", i);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","output":"1\n2\n3","explanation":"i increases after each iteration. When i becomes 4, the condition is false and the loop stops."},
   {"id":"cl3","type":"exercise","title":"Sum numbers from 1 to n","instruction":"Read an integer n and print the sum of all integers from 1 through n. Assume n is between 1 and 1000.","starterCode":"#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int n, sum = 0;\n    // Read n and calculate the sum\n    return 0;\n}","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int n, sum = 0;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) == 1) {\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) sum += i;\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","tests":[{"input":"5","expected":"15","check":"prints_sum_1_to_n"}],"explanation":"Start sum at zero and add each integer from 1 to n."},
   {"id":"cl4","type":"quiz","title":"Loop condition","question":"How many times does for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) execute its body?","options":["3","4","5","Infinite times"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The values are 0, 1, 2, and 3: four iterations."}
  ]
 },
 {
  "id":"c-arrays-functions-pointers","title":"Arrays, Functions and Pointers","track":"c","description":"Work with collections, reusable functions, and memory addresses.","difficulty":"intermediate","xpReward":90,"prerequisites":["c-conditions-and-loops"],
  "steps":[
   {"id":"cp1","type":"explain","title":"Arrays and indexes","content":"An array stores a fixed number of elements of the same type in consecutive positions. C indexes begin at zero. For an array of length n, valid indexes are 0 through n - 1; accessing outside this range causes undefined behaviour.","code":"int marks[3] = {70, 80, 90};\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", marks[0]);\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", marks[2]);","output":"70\n90","explanation":"marks[0] is the first element and marks[2] is the third."},
   {"id":"cp2","type":"explain","title":"Functions and return values","content":"A function groups reusable work. Its declaration (prototype) tells the compiler the function's name, return type, and parameters. A return statement sends a value back to the caller.","code":"#include <stdio.h>\nint add(int a, int b);\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", add(3, 4));\n    return 0;\n}\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }","output":"7","explanation":"add receives two integers and returns their sum."},
   {"id":"cp3","type":"explain","title":"Understand pointers","content":"A pointer stores the memory address of another object. The address-of operator & obtains a variable's address. The dereference operator * accesses the value at the address. Only dereference a pointer when it points to a valid object.","code":"#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int value = 10;\n    int *ptr = &value;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *ptr);\n    return 0;\n}","output":"10","explanation":"ptr stores the address of value. *ptr reads the integer stored at that address."},
   {"id":"cp4","type":"quiz","title":"Array bounds","question":"For int values[5], which is the last valid index?","options":["5","4","1","-1"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A five-element C array has indexes 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4."}
  ]
 }
